1. Core CLI Commands
    - Terraform provides a set of core commands for initializing, planning, applying, validating, and managing infrastructure.
    Command	                        Purpose
    terraform init	                Initializes the working directory and downloads required providers and modules.
    terraform plan	                Creates an execution plan showing what Terraform will add, change, or destroy.
    terraform apply	                Applies the planned changes to create or update infrastructure.
    terraform apply -auto-approve	Applies changes without asking for interactive confirmation.
    terraform destroy	            Removes all infrastructure resources managed by the current configuration.
    terraform fmt	                Formats Terraform configuration files according to standard style conventions.
    terraform validate	            Checks configuration files for syntax and internal consistency.
    terraform show	                Displays the current state or a saved execution plan in human-readable form.
    terraform state list	        Lists all resources currently tracked in the Terraform state file.
    terraform workspace show	    Displays the name of the currently selected workspace.

2. Examples
    # Initialize working directory (downloads providers)
    terraform init

    # Preview changes
    terraform plan

    # Apply changes
    terraform apply

    # Apply without interactive prompt
    terraform apply -auto-approve

    # Destroy all managed resources
    terraform destroy

    # Format code to canonical style
    terraform fmt

    # Validate configuration syntax
    terraform validate

    # Show current state
    terraform show

    # List resources in state
    terraform state list

    # Output current workspace
    terraform workspace show

3. Typical Terraform Workflow
    terraform init
    terraform fmt
    terraform validate
    terraform plan
    terraform apply